# Technical Debt

This is all the technical debt that exists within _SquirrelJME_ along with
the potential solution to solving it, which generally should be a simple
process with the caveat of being a long and drawn out repetitive task.

Any repetitive technical debt could be solved with a script or IntelliJ
replace intentions.

# Error Code Migration

The error codes in the form of `"NARG"` and `"AA01"` take up memory to store
strings and otherwise in classes, which just causes more static memory usage
than is actually needed. NanoCoat uses simplified error codes. The reason these
error codes were made is that in the early days of the virtual machine it
was very difficult to debug crashes and stack traces with a library that was
barely implemented. This effectively acted as a system that could be used to
reverse to the exact file and line where an error happened. This though is
useful to find issues, however it could be made to be simpler and more
efficient in both memory and space.

## Potential Ideas

Go through classes and rewrite `invokestatic` to a special method
such as `public static int __seq__()` to instead be `bipush`/`sipush` and
sequentially replace this within the class. Each one could be then placed in
a CSV for the file and line, along with the nearest description or
otherwise. The description could be in a comment or annotation. For comments,
this would depend on a SquirrelJME-specific proprietary Javadoc to get comments
to lines if they were to be Javadoc tags. Annotations could be associated
with the `__seq__()` call. Naturally, instructions at a given index can get
their line determined by the `LineNumberTable` attribute in the code.

